{% set local_problem %}
<p>Some <a href="/recent-projects/">Cullman properties</a> face drainage challenges that surface solutions can't fully address. Lots sloping toward the house, areas with higher water tables near Smith Lake, or properties where surface extensions would cross walkways, patios, or established landscaping all benefit from underground drainage.</p>

<p>For homeowners maintaining meticulous landscaping — and Cullman has many — underground drains offer another advantage: no visible extensions, splash blocks, or flex hoses interrupting carefully designed outdoor spaces. Water simply disappears underground and emerges far from the home.</p>

<p><a href="/recent-projects/">Properties around Smith Lake</a> may face additional challenges with humidity and elevated water tables. Underground drains can be essential for protecting foundations in these higher-moisture environments.</p>

<p><strong>The investment perspective:</strong> Underground drains for a typical Cullman home run $1,200-$2,400. Foundation repair runs $10,000-30,000. We've watched homeowners spend years dealing with moisture problems, trying cheaper fixes, before finally installing underground drains and solving the issue permanently. Should've done it first.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Underground drains are buried pipes connecting to your downspouts and carrying water through sealed channels to a discharge point far from your foundation. The system works by gravity — no pumps, no electricity, no moving parts to fail.</p>

<p><strong>What we install:</strong> 4-inch Schedule 40 PVC pipe with smooth interior walls. Proper slope (1-2% grade) ensuring water flows continuously and doesn't pool in the pipe. Pop-up emitters or daylight exits at discharge points. Clean-out access for future maintenance.</p>

<p><strong>What we don't install:</strong> Corrugated flex pipe. This is critical. That black corrugated drainage pipe from Home Depot seems like a cheap alternative, but the corrugations trap sediment and debris. Roots grow into the ridges. Within 3-5 years, most corrugated pipe is partially or completely clogged.</p>

<p>We dig up failed corrugated systems constantly — homeowners thought they solved the problem, but created a new one. Smooth PVC costs more upfront but actually works for decades.</p>

<p><strong>The distinction:</strong> Underground gutter drains handle roof runoff. French drains handle groundwater already in soil around your foundation. Different systems for different problems. Some Cullman properties need both. We assess what's actually happening with your water and recommend accordingly.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've installed underground drainage throughout Cullman County for over 20 years. We design systems that solve problems permanently, use quality materials that last, and take care to minimize disruption to your property.</p>

<p>We'll assess your situation honestly. If simpler solutions will work, we'll say so — no point spending $2,000 on underground drains if $200 in extensions would fix the problem. If underground drains are the right choice, we'll explain why and design a system meeting your specific needs.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If you're spending money on underground drainage, spend the extra 20-30% for smooth PVC. The corrugated stuff isn't cheaper when you account for the fact that it fails and needs replacement within 3-5 years. This is one place where "saving money" costs more long-term.</p>

